<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suffolk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suffolk is a county of East Anglia in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipswich">Ipswich</a> as its county town. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> its land was divided between the Iceni and the Trinovantes, and from the 5th century it was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and formed the southern part of the kingdom of East Anglia; its name means 'south folk'. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> royal burial ground of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suffolk.jpg" alt="Suffolk" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexey Komarov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orford_Ness_2016-08-27-2_(cropped,_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
